  "id": "Compound::DB01069 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Promethazine is linked to Chrm3 through a series of molecular and functional interactions that involve the Hrh4 protein. The evidence suggests that Promethazine binds to Hrh4, which then participates in receptor activity related to acetylcholine, whether through neurotransmitter or G-protein coupled pathways. This indicates that Hrh4 acts as a bridge between Promethazine and the cholinergic receptor Chrm3, facilitating a signaling cascade. The overlapping roles of Hrh4 in different receptor activities suggest a general mechanism by which Promethazine may influence cholinergic signaling, potentially modulating receptor function or activity. The consistent involvement of Hrh4 in receptor-related processes supports a unified pathway where Promethazine exerts its effects through Hrh4, ultimately impacting Chrm3.",
